# vllm_openai_xpu/.env.example — copy to `.env` (auto-read by Compose, gitignored).
#
# This engine alternates between TWO validated models. Uncomment EXACTLY ONE of
# the two blocks below, then force-recreate:
#
#   docker compose -f vllm_openai_xpu/compose.yaml up -d --force-recreate
#
# --force-recreate is mandatory: vLLM bakes its CLI args into the container, so
# a plain `up -d` silently keeps the previous model's arguments.
#
# !! SWITCH A WHOLE BLOCK AT ONCE. Three variables are tied to the model and
#    fail in three different ways if carried across:
#      - VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY is ABSOLUTE bytes and OOMs rather than shrinking.
#        gemma-4 weights (16.93 GiB) + gpt-oss's 8.0 GiB pin > 22.33 GiB free
#        => boot OOM. The reverse boots but strands ~4 GiB and halves the pool.
#      - VLLM_EAGER_FLAG: --no-enforce-eager with gpt-oss's 8.6 GiB pin STARVES
#        the KV pool and the boot dies. It is the PIN that decides this, not the
#        context size: gemma-4 runs compiled at 131072 fine on its 4.25 GiB pin.
#      - VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN DIFFERS between the blocks (A=131072, B=32768), so
#        carrying it across silently caps the other model's context. Always set
#        it per block.
#    A mismatched reasoning/tool parser does NOT crash; it quietly yields an
#    empty reasoning field or no tool_calls. Easy to miss.
#    Leaving a block fully commented is always safe: every variable falls back
#    to the compose default, which is the gpt-oss-20b block.
#
# !! SINGLE GPU — `down` the other engine folder first (vllm_xpu/ or scaler/).
#
# To run the OTHER block temporarily WITHOUT editing this file, use the shell
# override block in ./README.md ("Switching models -> Temporary"). Shell vars
# beat .env, but ONLY for the names you pass — every variable the block sets
# must be overridden, including VLLM_EAGER_FLAG and VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY.

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BLOCK A — gpt-oss-20b   (compose default; best for long / cold contexts)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Measured on this B60: 12.87 GiB weights, 83.2 tok/s (bench.sh chunk count),
# 338,928-token KV pool = 2.59x concurrency @128k.
# Reasoning is ALWAYS on and has no off switch, so VLLM_DEFAULT_CHAT_TEMPLATE_-
# KWARGS is left unset here (an unknown template kwarg is verified harmless).
# EAGER IS REQUIRED by this block's 8.6 GiB pin — do not add VLLM_EAGER_FLAG.
# Still the better choice for coding-CLI traffic via a gateway: gpt-oss's prefill
# stays LINEAR at 131,072, whereas gemma-4 would need ~92 min to cold-fill that
# much -- which is exactly why Block B ships capped at 32768.
#
#VLLM_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
#VLLM_SERVED_MODEL_NAME=gpt-oss-20b
#VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=openai_gptoss
#VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=openai
#VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=131072
#VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=8603448832

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BLOCK B — gemma-4-26B-A4B-it, offline int4  (newer model, vision-capable)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Measured on this B60: 16.93 GiB weights, TTFT ~105 ms,
# 78,433-token KV pool = 2.39x concurrency @32,768.
#
# CONTEXT IS NOT VRAM-LIMITED HERE, AND 32768 WAS A MISTAKE. 25 of gemma-4's 30
# layers are sliding-attention (window 1024) and their KV does NOT scale with
# context; only the 5 full-attention layers do, at 20 KiB/token. MEASURED at the
# SAME 4.25 GiB pin: 32768 -> 2.39x | 65536 -> 1.77x | 131072 -> 1.16x, with
# weights, pool, decode and TTFT all unchanged. 131072 boots, serves and passes
# smoke.sh. Hard ceiling is 162,496. An earlier revision claimed "~48.5k" --
# that was a 4x head-count arithmetic error PLUS a 64k boot attempt that also
# raised --max-num-batched-tokens. Do NOT raise that flag: it feeds
# max_in_flight_tokens, inflates the SLIDING reservation, and at 8192 it
# collapses the ceiling to 48,576. UPSTREAM_VLLM_NOTES.md §11
#
# WHY 32768 AND NOT MORE -- it is a GUARDRAIL, not a memory limit, and NOT a
# speed setting. MEASURED: decode and TTFT are IDENTICAL at 32k / 64k / 128k
# (46.2 / 46.3 / 46.3 tok/s, TTFT 105-106 ms), the pool is 4.25 GiB at every
# cap, and a given prompt size costs the same whatever the cap is. Lowering the
# cap does NOT make the model faster.
# What the cap DOES control is the LONGEST PREFILL A CLIENT CAN TRIGGER:
#   32768 -> ~4.6 min | 65536 -> ~22 min | 98304 -> ~51 min | 131072 -> ~92 min
# At 32768 an oversized prompt fails fast with a 400 instead of occupying the
# B60 for tens of minutes and then probably timing out upstream anyway. It also
# leaves the most room for prefix cache (2.39x vs 1.77x at 64k).
# Raise it when you have a use for prompts that big AND timeouts along the whole
# path to match. 131072 is verified working; ceiling is 162,496.
#
# CHECKPOINT CONSTRAINT: must be int4-symmetric with group_size 32 or
# channelwise. The XPU expert kernel (XPUExpertsWNA16) accepts only those two
# schemes, so the smaller and far more popular group-64 builds are REJECTED at
# load despite being ~2 GiB lighter. Check
# quantization_config.config_groups.*.weights.group_size before trying another.
#
# PREFILL IS SUPER-QUADRATIC and NOT tunable -- this, not VRAM, is the real
# limit on usable context. gemma-4 has heterogeneous head dims (sliding 256 /
# full-attention 512); XPU has no FA4 kernel, so TRITON_ATTN is force-selected
# at config level and VLLM_ATTENTION_BACKEND is ignored.
# Measured cold, one harness, one config: 9,643 -> 26.3 s | 31,957 -> 305.3 s |
# 64,708 -> 1297.6 s (21.6 min, 49.9 tok/s prefill -- barely faster than DECODE
# at ~46 tok/s, which is the tell that the Triton kernel wastes prefill's
# parallelism). Least-squares exponent 2.048 (plain quadratic), reproducing all
# three within 0.1%:  T ~= 26.3 s * (n/9643)^2.048
# => ~4.6 min at the 32768 cap, ~22 min at 64k, ~92 min at 128k.
# Neither --max-num-batched-tokens nor compiled mode helps.
# PREFIX CACHING DOES, and it IMPROVES with size (measured cold -> warm):
#   9,643 tok  26.3 s -> 0.31 s  =  84x
#   31,957 tok 305.3 s -> 0.63 s = 481x
# Warm time stays roughly flat while cold grows quadratically. So conversation
# and growing context are fine; a large COLD context (fresh RAG doc, big paste)
# is where it hurts, and you pay it once per document per engine lifetime -- the
# cache lives in VRAM and does NOT survive a container recreate.
#
VLLM_MODEL=reinforce20001/gemma4-26b-a4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct
VLLM_SERVED_MODEL_NAME=gemma-4-26b-a4b
VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=gemma4
VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=gemma4
VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=131072
VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=4563402752

# Compiled mode instead of eager. MEASURED faster than eager with the 4.25 GiB
# pin above INTACT and no OOM -- verified at 32768, 65536 AND 131072, because
# the compile buffers scale with --max-num-batched-tokens (2496), NOT context.
# (The old "+7.1%, 52.0 -> 55.7 tok/s" figures did not reproduce on 2026-09-17,
# which measured ~46 tok/s at every context length; the eager-vs-compiled
# ranking still holds, the absolute numbers need re-measuring.)
# Costs ~34 s of torch.compile on every boot (the SYCL cache must stay off, so
# it never persists) and does NOT help prefill.
# Passed as the WHOLE flag: argparse builds this as a BooleanOptionalAction, so
# `--enforce-eager=False` does not parse — it must be `--no-enforce-eager`.
# !! DO NOT carry this into Block A: eager is REQUIRED BY GPT-OSS'S 8.6 GiB
# PIN (not by context size -- gemma runs compiled at 131072 fine). With that pin
# the compile buffers, which gpu-memory-utilization does not cap, starve the
# KV pool and the boot dies.
VLLM_EAGER_FLAG=--no-enforce-eager

# gemma-4's reasoning is OPT-IN — its chat template defaults enable_thinking to
# false, unlike gpt-oss which always reasons. This turns it on server-side so
# clients need no special field. VERIFIED on this box:
#   unset/null   -> ./smoke.sh reasoning check FAILS, reasoning len 0
#   set as below -> ./smoke.sh PASSES with no client flag, reasoning len 738
# A request still overrides it BOTH ways (also verified):
#   "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}   -> suppresses it
# It does NOT cost decode speed (~52 tok/s either way, measured by
# usage.completion_tokens — bench.sh's lower figure with reasoning on is an
# artifact of counting SSE chunks). Its real cost is BUDGET: reasoning consumes
# max_tokens first, so at 400 you get a trace and no answer. Allow 1500+.
# Comment out to go back to per-request opt-in.
#VLLM_DEFAULT_CHAT_TEMPLATE_KWARGS={"enable_thinking":true}

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# After any switch
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The served model name changes with the block, so DOWNSTREAM BREAKS until it is
# repointed: an AI gateway (LiteLLM, Bifrost — any gateway) maps a fixed model
# id and will 404 against the other block. Repoint the consumer's model mapping.
# The blocks also differ in CONTEXT CAP (A=131,072, B=32,768) and cost far more
# differently than that ratio suggests: do NOT serve one model under the other's
# name. Repoint the consumer's context cap as well as its model id. Keep names
# truthful, one per model.
#
# Verify. Both scripts auto-detect the served model when MODEL is unset, print
# the real checkpoint behind the label, and take a tri-state THINKING
# (auto = report what the DEPLOYED default does):
#   ./smoke.sh              # ALL PASS on both blocks as configured above
#   THINKING=1 ./smoke.sh   # force reasoning on  -> expects a trace
#   THINKING=0 ./smoke.sh   # force reasoning off -> expects it SUPPRESSED
#   ./bench.sh 400          # ~83 tok/s gpt-oss (chunks), ~46 gemma (compiled)
#   ./bench.sh 1500         # with reasoning on, needs the larger budget

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Engine knobs — shared by both blocks, rarely changed
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MUST stay 0 while VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1: 1 segfaults at boot inside the
# SYCL persistent code-cache read. Costs a kernel rebuild each boot (~70 s).
#SYCL_CACHE_PERSISTENT=0

# Upstream default from 0.29.0 on; measured faster and leaner here. 0 pins V1.
# Integer only — a blank value crashes at boot.
#VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1

# Compiled vs eager. Default --enforce-eager is required by Block A's 8.6 GiB
# pin; --no-enforce-eager is safe for Block B at any context up to 131,072
# (boot-tested), because compile buffers scale with max-num-batched-tokens.
#VLLM_EAGER_FLAG=--enforce-eager

# XPU graph. Only does anything in COMPILED mode — a genuine no-op under eager,
# which is the only way it had been tested before. In compiled mode it DOES
# capture (51 PIECEWISE + 35 FULL graphs) but costs 1.54 GiB, dropping the pool
# 78,433 -> 46,138 tokens and concurrency 2.39x -> 1.41x for +0.5% (measured at
# 32k; the 1.54 GiB cost is context-independent, so it bites harder the larger
# the cap). Not worth it.
#VLLM_XPU_ENABLE_XPU_GRAPH=0

# Empty = anonymous. Only needed for gated repos; neither block above is gated.
#HF_TOKEN=
